BT419.52 52.56  TD0 0 0 rg /F1 9.12  Tf0.0785  Tc -0.0497  Tw (Questionnaire Appraisal System ) Tj90.72 -10.8  TD -0.0864  Tc 0  Tw (QAS-99) Tj-69.36 -10.8  TD 0.0518  Tc -0.023  Tw (Research Triangle Institute) Tj-143.04 0.72  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw (3-2) Tj-225.84 677.04  TD ( ) Tj0 -17.76  TD (2. ) Tj36 0  TD /F3 12  Tf-0.04  Tc 0.04  Tw (\223There are problems with every question.\224) Tj0 -35.52  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw (This reaction is common because there is no such thing as a perfect survey question, and) Tj-36 -17.76  TD (the QAS-99 is designed to find a lot of different types of potential flaws.  So, instead of just) Tj0 -17.76  TD (concluding that it is futile to script good questions, it may help to consider a few points that put) Tj0 -17.52  TD (the appraisal process in the appropriate context:  ) Tj36 -35.52  TD /F5 12  Tf(!) Tj36 0  TD /F1 12  Tf(Using a system such as this to critique the question in effect forces the user) Tj0 -14.16  TD (to consider many aspects of the question and to search extensively for) TjT* (problems.  Under such a microscope, problems will likely be found) TjT* (frequently.  It may not be possible to fix everything.  If the most basic) TjT* (problems can be identified, and addressed, however, the exercise has been) TjT* (worthwhile.) Tj-36 -28.32  TD /F5 12  Tf(!) Tj36 0  TD /F1 12  Tf(Problems differ in their seriousness.  Whereas some may simply make the) Tj0 -14.16  TD (question less than ideal for purposes of analysis, others could render the) TjT* (information obtained completely useless.  The appraiser should consider) TjT* (the ramifications of the problems found, given the intended purposes of the) TjT* (question.  Sometimes we have to ) Tj-1.68  Tc (\223) Tj0  Tc (live with) Tj-1.44  Tc 1.44  Tw (\224 ) Tj0  Tc 0  Tw (questions that have some) TjT* (degree of vagueness because we cannot specify terms to the extent that) TjT* (legal text does.  Or, the topic may be sensitive in nature because there is no) TjT* (way to escape the fact that some risk behaviors involve such behaviors as) TjT* (sex and drug use.  The fact that a question may contain one or more) TjT* (potential problems does not mean we should absolutely not ask it.  Rather,) TjT* (we should recognize that there is no such thing as a perfect question, and) TjT* (attempt to develop the best form possible, consider the limitations, and) TjT* (make a decision regarding the worthiness of administering that question.) Tj-36 -28.32  TD /F5 12  Tf(!) Tj36 0  TD /F1 12  Tf(Reasonable people can disagree about which problem type applies, or) Tj0 -14.16  TD (whether a problem even exists.  A degree of subjectivity is inherent in all) TjT* (question appraisals.  We have no evidence other than our own experience) TjT* (and judgment to use in deciding whether respondents will know or) TjT* (remember certain information, whether a question is too long, or whether) TjT* (the response categories used are meaningful to the majority of the) TjT* (population surveyed.  Such decisions may be further confirmed or refuted) TjT* (by pretesting.   Through question appraisal, we simply do our best to make) TjT* (a number of these subjective judgments, and to create the best questions) TjT* (that are possible at this point.  The QAS-99 is a tool that guides us through) TjT* (this process.) Tj-36 -14.16  TD (In summary, remember that the overall purpose of the QAS-99 is not to find ways to) Tj-36 -17.76  TD (criticize every survey question \(or questionnaire designer!\), but rather, to help the designer to find) TjET
endstreamendobj55 0 obj<</Length 4400>>stream
