 Practice) Tj36 -35.52  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw (QAS-99 is a tool for you to use to evaluate and revise draft questions. If one diligently) Tj-36 -17.76  TD (applies the QAS-99, few questions may escape being assigned a problem code because no) Tj0 -17.76  TD (question is perfect for every respondent that will be encountered in a survey. Your goal is to) TjT* (understand the potential problems and to reduce them as much as possible.  Thus, we recommend) Tj0 -17.52  TD (that you be strict in assigning codes and err on the side of finding a problem rather than not) Tj0 -17.76  TD (finding any.  Otherwise, you will miss opportunities to consider more effective wordings and) TjT* (structures for the questions that you are drafting.  ) Tj36 -35.28  TD (You can use the QAS-99 in several ways:) Tj-36 -35.76  TD (1.) Tj36 0  TD /F0 12  Tf-0.0327  Tc 0.1527  Tw (Improving questions) Tj105.36 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw (:  You can use the QAS-99 to guide the revision of your own) Tj-105.36 -17.76  TD (questions after you write them.  Keep in mind that even after learning the system, and) Tj0 -17.76  TD (considering how to avoid various pitfalls, it is still important to apply the QAS-99, and to) Tj0 -17.52  TD (do the question coding, rather than just concluding that ) Tj-1.68  Tc (\223) Tj0  Tc (now that I know what to avoid,) Tj0 -17.76  TD (I) Tj-2.88  Tc (\222) Tj0  Tc (ll write good questions.) Tj-0.03  Tc 0.03  Tw (\224  Even very expert questionnaire designers benefit from) TjT* 0  Tc 0  Tw (considering a number of separate aspects of a question) Tj5.28  Tc (\227) Tj0  Tc (systematically, and one at a) TjT* (time) Tj5.28  Tc (\227) Tj0  Tc (and the QAS-99 forces one to do this.  So, after you have drafted questions, use) Tj0 -17.52  TD (the QAS-99 to identify potential problems with them, then revise the questionnaire to) Tj0 -17.76  TD (remove these potential problems. ) TjET
