l systems and, in) Tj0 -17.76  TD (part, from a method developed to examine and classify the cognitive processes inherent in the) TjT* (question-answering process ) Tj134.88 0  TD /F1 11.04  Tf-0.012  Tc 0.0216  Tw (\(Lessler and Forsyth, 1996\)) Tj120.96 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw (.) Tj3.12 4.8  TD /F1 7.2  Tf(1) Tj3.6 -4.8  TD /F1 12  Tf(   This version is different from ones that) Tj-262.56 -17.76  TD (we have used in other studies.  In this version, we have eliminated codes that characterize the) Tj0 -17.52  TD (nature of the response task, that focus on mnemonic and judgment processes, and have, instead,) Tj0 -17.76  TD (focused on question characteristics that are likely to present problems when administered in the) TjT* (field.) Tj0 -36.48  TD /F0 12.96  Tf-0.0921  Tc 0.2121  Tw (Overview of the QAS) Tj36 -35.76  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw (The QAS guides users through a systematic appraisal of survey questions and helps them) Tj-36 -17.76  TD (to spot potential problems in the wording or structure of the questions that may lead to difficulties) Tj0 -17.76  TD (in question administration, miscommunication, or other failings.  The user examines proposed) Tj0 -17.52  TD (questions by considering specific categories of question characteristics in a step-wise fashion and,) Tj0 -17.76  TD (at each step, decides whether the question exhibits features that are likely to cause problems.  In) TjT* (completing the appraisal, the user indicates whether  the problem is present by circling YES or) TjT* (NO on an accompanying coding form \(see Section 2\) and, for each YES circled, notes the reason) Tj0 -17.52  TD (a YES code was assigned.   In brief, the steps are as follows, with a full discussion of each step) Tj0 -18  TD (given in the ) Tj58.32 0  TD /F0 12  Tf0.016  Tc 0.104  Tw (QAS-99 Manual) Tj84 0  TD /F1 12  Tf0  Tc 0  Tw ( \(Section 3\):) TjET
